---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro'
import { externalLinks } from '../../data/links.jsx'

const pageDescription = 'Voting Handbook'

const sectionClass =
  'mt-6 w-full scroll-mt-24 rounded-lg border border-[color:var(--theme-color-150)] bg-[color:var(--theme-special-lightest)] p-6 shadow-sm'

const navLinks = [
  { href: '#before-election', label: 'Before the elections are announced' },
  { href: '#after-election', label: 'After elections are announced' },
  { href: '#election-day', label: 'On election day' },
  { href: '#faq', label: 'Frequently asked questions' },
]
---

<BaseLayout lang="en" pageDescription={pageDescription}>
  <article class="relative flex flex-col items-center justify-center">
    <h1
      class="title mt-6 bg-gradient-to-r from-accent-two/85 via-accent-one/85 to-accent-two/85 bg-clip-text text-center !text-2xl text-transparent dark:from-accent-two dark:via-accent-one dark:to-accent-two sm:mt-0 sm:text-4xl"
    >
      Voting Abroad
    </h1>

    <section class="mt-6 w-full">
      <p>
        Serbia is, according to its constitution, a democratic republic, and
        elections are the fundamental way for citizens to choose their
        representatives and influence the governance of the country. The right
        to vote belongs to all adult citizens of the Republic of Serbia,
        including those who live abroad temporarily or permanently.
      </p>
      <p>
        To exercise their voting right from abroad, citizens must be registered
        in the Unified Voters' Register and, after the elections are called,
        submit a request to vote from abroad. This request must be submitted
        every time, regardless of whether you have voted abroad before.
      </p>
      <p>
        This handbook explains, step by step, what needs to be done before and
        after the elections are called, as well as on election day itself, so
        that the diaspora can exercise its right to vote simply and on time.
      </p>

      <nav
        aria-label="Handbook contents"
        class="mt-4 flex flex-wrap justify-center gap-2 text-sm"
      >
        {
          navLinks.map((link) => (
            <a
              href={link.href}
              class="rounded-full border border-[color:var(--theme-color-150)] px-3 py-1.5 font-medium text-accent-two no-underline transition-colors hover:bg-[color:var(--theme-color-100)]"
            >
              {link.label}
            </a>
          ))
        }
      </nav>
    </section>

    <section id="before-election" class={sectionClass}>
      <h2 class="title mb-4 text-xl text-accent-two">
        Before the elections are announced
      </h2>

      <h3 class="text-l text-accent-three title mb-2 mt-6 sm:mt-4">
        Voting eligibility
      </h3>
      <p>
        The right to vote is granted to citizens of the Republic of Serbia who
        possess valid documents (a national ID card or a passport of the
        Republic of Serbia).
      </p>
      <p>
        Voting from abroad applies to parliamentary elections and presidential
        elections. Local elections (for city and municipal councils) can only be
        voted in at the polling station of your registered residence in Serbia.
      </p>
      <p>
        If your passport has expired, you must personally submit a request for
        the issuance of a new one at the nearest diplomatic or consular mission
        of the Republic of Serbia. Along with the request, you should submit the
        old passport (or an ID card, or another public document that can be used
        to verify your identity, if you do not have the old passport). According
        to the procedure, a birth certificate is also required (as well as proof
        of citizenship of the Republic of Serbia, if you have never had a
        biometric passport before), but this is obtained ex officio – so you do
        not need to worry about that. The legal deadline for processing the
        request is 60 days, so we urge you not to delay in submitting it, as the
        procedure is fairly lengthy.
      </p>
      <p>
        More info: <a
          class="text-accent-two underline-offset-2 hover:underline"
          href={externalLinks.mfa_passport}
          target="_blank"
          rel="noopener noreferrer"
        >
          mfa.gov.rs
        </a>
      </p>

      <h3 class="text-l text-accent-three title mb-2 mt-6 sm:mt-4">
        Check the electoral roll
      </h3>
      <p>
        In order to exercise your right to vote, you must be registered in the
        Unified Voters' Register. You can check your registration status via the
        following link: <a
          class="text-accent-two underline-offset-2 hover:underline"
          href={externalLinks.electoral_roll}
          target="_blank"
          rel="noopener noreferrer"
        >
          upit.birackispisak.gov.rs
        </a>
      </p>
      <p>
        If you are not listed in the voters' register, you can submit a
        registration request only after the elections have been called.
      </p>

      <h3 class="text-l text-accent-three title mb-2 mt-6 sm:mt-4">
        Check Serbian Diplomatic Missions Worldwide
      </h3>
      <p>
        You can check them via the following link: <a
          class="text-accent-two underline-offset-2 hover:underline"
          href={externalLinks.mfa_embassy}
          target="_blank"
          rel="noopener noreferrer"
        >
          Serbian Diplomatic Missions
        </a>
      </p>

      <h3 class="text-l text-accent-three title mb-2 mt-6 sm:mt-4">
        What if there is no Serbian embassy or consulate in my country?
      </h3>
      <p>
        If there is no Serbian embassy or consulate in the country where you
        reside, you can still exercise your voting right – but with additional
        steps:
      </p>
      <h4 class="title mb-2 mt-4">
        1. Traveling to the nearest diplomatic-consular mission
      </h4>
      <p>
        The most common solution is to travel to the nearest country where a
        Serbian diplomatic-consular mission exists. For example, if you live in
        Iceland (which has no embassy), the nearest location is the Embassy in
        Oslo. In this case, when applying to vote, you indicate that location as
        your voting place.
      </p>
      <p>
        If there is no Serbian mission in your country, the request form on this
        site automatically detects this and offers you the address of the
        responsible mission that covers your country – you do not need to look
        it up yourself.
      </p>
      <h4 class="title mb-2 mt-4">
        2. Traveling to Serbia and voting within the Republic
      </h4>
      <p>
        If you did not register to vote abroad or your nearest embassy is
        unavailable, you can vote in Serbia – but you must be listed in the
        electoral roll in Serbia and have valid identification.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-4 font-medium text-[color:var(--theme-accent)]">
        For a polling station to be opened at an embassy or consulate, a minimum
        of 100 registered voters is required. If not enough people register –
        that polling station will not be opened, and all voters will need to
        vote at another location.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section id="after-election" class={sectionClass}>
      <h2 class="title mb-4 text-xl text-accent-two">
        After elections are announced
      </h2>

      <h3 class="text-l text-accent-three title mb-2 mt-6 sm:mt-4">
        Registering in the electoral roll
      </h3>
      <p>
        A request for registration in the Unified Voters' Register should be
        submitted only if you are not already registered. Diplomatic and
        consular missions are required to publish the request form on their
        website. The request must be sent via email to the respective diplomatic
        or consular mission no later than five days before the closing of the
        Unified Voters' Register.
      </p>
      <p>
        More information can be found in the <a
          class="text-accent-two underline-offset-2 hover:underline"
          href={externalLinks.unified_voters_law}
          target="_blank"
          rel="noopener noreferrer"
        >
          Law on the Unified Voters' Register
        </a> (“Official Gazette of RS”, Nos. 104/2009, 99/2011, and 44/2024).
      </p>

      <h3 class="text-l text-accent-three title mb-2 mt-6 sm:mt-4">
        Voting from abroad
      </h3>
      <p>
        A request to register the information in the Voters' Register indicating
        that a voter will vote abroad must be submitted in order to vote from
        abroad. Diplomatic and consular missions are required to publish the
        request form on their website. The request must be sent via email to the
        respective diplomatic or consular mission no later than five days before
        the closing of the Unified Voters' Register. The request must be
        submitted regardless of whether you have voted abroad in the past.
      </p>
      <p>
        More information can be found in the <a
          class="text-accent-two underline-offset-2 hover:underline"
          href={externalLinks.unified_voters_law}
          target="_blank"
          rel="noopener noreferrer"
        >
          Law on the Unified Voters' Register
        </a> (“Official Gazette of RS”, Nos. 104/2009, 99/2011, and 44/2024).
      </p>

      <h3 class="text-l text-accent-three title mb-2 mt-6 sm:mt-4">
        Request for voter information
      </h3>
      <p>
        Submitting a request for notification about the data entered in the
        Voters' Register regarding a voter is not mandatory, but it is highly
        recommended, as it increases your chances of receiving feedback
        confirming that you can vote abroad. Otherwise, the competent
        authorities are not legally obligated to inform you of any changes in
        the Voters' Register. There is no official form for this request; it is
        sufficient to send an email (ideally using the same email used for the
        previously mentioned requests), asking to receive information about any
        changes in the Voters' Register based on Paragraph 4 of Article 16 of
        the law referenced in the following paragraph. The request must be sent
        to the diplomatic or consular mission no later than five days before the
        closing of the Unified Voters' Register.
      </p>
      <p>
        More information can be found in the <a
          class="text-accent-two underline-offset-2 hover:underline"
          href={externalLinks.unified_voters_law}
          target="_blank"
          rel="noopener noreferrer"
        >
          Law on the Unified Voters' Register
        </a> (“Official Gazette of RS”, Nos. 104/2009, 99/2011, and 44/2024).
      </p>

      <h3 class="text-l text-accent-three title mb-2 mt-6 sm:mt-4">
        Follow up on requests
      </h3>
      <p>
        To ensure that your request has been received, you should check every
        day until the deadline for submission. You can contact the
        diplomatic-consular mission via email, phone, or by visiting in person.
      </p>
      <p>
        It is advisable that people from the same location keep a record of who
        has submitted requests, so that it is clear whether the quota for
        establishing a polling station has been met.
      </p>

      <h3 class="text-l text-accent-three title mb-2 mt-6 sm:mt-4">
        Polling station determination
      </h3>
      <p>
        A polling station may be established for a minimum of 100 and a maximum
        of 2,500 voters. In exceptional cases, with the consent of the Republic
        Electoral Commission (RIK), a polling station may also be established
        for fewer than 100 voters if, due to distance or unfavorable geographic
        conditions, it would be significantly difficult for the residents of a
        specific area to vote at another polling station, or for more than 2,500
        voters if there is no physical capacity to open additional polling
        stations.
      </p>
      <p>
        The Ministry of Foreign Affairs submits to RIK a proposal for
        establishing polling stations abroad no later than 22 days before
        election day. RIK then issues a decision on the polling stations no
        later than 20 days before election day. Since the voters' register is
        finalized 15 days before election day, if the proposed list of polling
        stations is unsatisfactory, and there is legal basis to establish new or
        relocate existing stations, the issue should be made public immediately
        after RIK announces it on its website, in order to encourage amendments
        and/or additions.
      </p>
      <p>
        More information on how polling station proposals are determined, their
        accessibility, and the characteristics of the facilities can be found in
        the <a
          class="text-accent-two underline-offset-2 hover:underline"
          href={externalLinks.determining_polling_instructions}
          target="_blank"
          rel="noopener noreferrer"
        >
          Instructions on the Method of Determining Polling Stations
        </a> (“Official Gazette of RS”, No. 91/2023 – consolidated text). The following
        link contains the forms that the Ministry of Foreign Affairs must complete
        when submitting its proposal to RIK:
        <a
          class="text-accent-two underline-offset-2 hover:underline"
          href={externalLinks.eu_standards}
          target="_blank"
          rel="noopener noreferrer"
        >
          Ministry forms
        </a>
      </p>
    </section>

    <section id="election-day" class={sectionClass}>
      <h2 class="title mb-4 text-xl text-accent-two">On election day</h2>

      <h3 class="text-l text-accent-three title mb-2 mt-6 sm:mt-4">
        How voting works
      </h3>
      <p>
        Voting from abroad is in person and secret. On election day, you must
        personally go to the polling station (the embassy or consulate) you
        indicated in your request, with a valid ID document (national ID card or
        passport). Voting by mail or by proxy is not available. If you are not
        sure exactly where your polling station is, you can get that information
        from the diplomatic-consular mission where you submitted your request.
      </p>

      <h3 class="text-l text-accent-three title mb-2 mt-6 sm:mt-4">
        How to report irregularities
      </h3>
      <p>
        Some of the irregularities you may encounter at a polling station
        include:
      </p>
      <ul class="list-content list-inside list-disc" role="list">
        <li>
          presence of campaign materials at the polling station or within 50
          meters of it
        </li>
        <li>
          voting outside the polling station without a certificate granting the
          right to vote outside the polling station
        </li>
        <li>voting without a valid identity document</li>
        <li>
          failure to mark the voter's name in the voters' list or marking the
          name of another voter
        </li>
        <li>
          influencing the voter's choice by members of the polling board or
          third parties
        </li>
        <li>absence of the control slip in the ballot box</li>
        <li>
          more ballots in the box than the number of voters who have voted
        </li>
        <li>
          presence of police officers at the polling station without a request
          from the president of the polling board, unless public order and peace
          have been disturbed
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        If you notice any of the above irregularities, ask the members of the
        polling board to record it in the Official Report on the Work of the
        Polling Board.
      </p>
      <p>
        You can report the irregularity no later than 24 hours from the moment
        it occurred. More info: <a
          class="text-accent-two underline-offset-2 hover:underline"
          href={externalLinks.protection_guide}
          target="_blank"
          rel="noopener noreferrer"
        >
          Guide for the Protection of Voters' Electoral Rights
        </a>
      </p>

      <h3 class="text-l text-accent-three title mb-2 mt-6 sm:mt-4">
        Complaints about violations of electoral rights
      </h3>
      <p>
        If you believe your electoral right has been violated, or you notice an
        irregularity in the conduct of the election, a complaint must be
        submitted to the Republic Electoral Commission (RIK) no later than 24
        hours from the moment the disputed decision, action, or omission
        occurred. If you are not satisfied with RIK's decision on the complaint,
        you may file an appeal with the Administrative Court, which decides
        within 48 hours. A detailed guide to the procedure can be found in the <a
          class="text-accent-two underline-offset-2 hover:underline"
          href={externalLinks.protection_guide}
          target="_blank"
          rel="noopener noreferrer"
        >
          Guide for the Protection of Voters' Electoral Rights
        </a>.
      </p>

      <h3 class="text-l text-accent-three title mb-2 mt-6 sm:mt-4">
        Counting votes from abroad
      </h3>
      <p>
        Polling boards at polling stations abroad are composed of
        representatives of the electoral list proponents, just as at polling
        stations in Serbia. After the polling station closes, the polling board
        counts the votes and prepares a report, which is submitted to the
        Republic Electoral Commission. RIK publishes the combined results from
        all polling stations, including those abroad, on its website.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section id="faq" class={sectionClass}>
      <h2 class="title mb-4 text-xl text-accent-two">
        Frequently asked questions
      </h2>

      <div class="divide-y divide-[color:var(--theme-color-150)]">
        <details class="group py-3">
          <summary
            class="text-l text-accent-three title flex cursor-pointer list-none items-center justify-between gap-3 marker:content-none [&::-webkit-details-marker]:hidden"
          >
            I'm turning 18 soon – can I vote?
            <svg
              class="h-4 w-4 shrink-0 text-accent-two transition-transform group-open:rotate-180"
              xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"
              viewBox="0 0 24 24"
              fill="none"
              stroke="currentColor"
              stroke-width="2"
              stroke-linecap="round"
              stroke-linejoin="round"
            >
              <path d="m6 9 6 6 6-6"></path>
            </svg>
          </summary>
          <p class="mt-2">
            Yes. If you will be 18 years old by election day at the latest, you
            have the right to vote. The competent city or municipal
            administration registers you in the voters' register ex officio – no
            separate registration request is needed because of turning 18.
          </p>
        </details>

        <details class="group py-3">
          <summary
            class="text-l text-accent-three title flex cursor-pointer list-none items-center justify-between gap-3 marker:content-none [&::-webkit-details-marker]:hidden"
          >
            I have dual citizenship – can I vote?
            <svg
              class="h-4 w-4 shrink-0 text-accent-two transition-transform group-open:rotate-180"
              xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"
              viewBox="0 0 24 24"
              fill="none"
              stroke="currentColor"
              stroke-width="2"
              stroke-linecap="round"
              stroke-linejoin="round"
            >
              <path d="m6 9 6 6 6-6"></path>
            </svg>
          </summary>
          <p class="mt-2">
            Yes. If you hold citizenship of the Republic of Serbia and a valid
            Serbian document (national ID card or passport), you have the right
            to vote regardless of whether you also hold citizenship of another
            country.
          </p>
        </details>

        <details class="group py-3">
          <summary
            class="text-l text-accent-three title flex cursor-pointer list-none items-center justify-between gap-3 marker:content-none [&::-webkit-details-marker]:hidden"
          >
            I submitted a request but received no confirmation – what should I
            do?
            <svg
              class="h-4 w-4 shrink-0 text-accent-two transition-transform group-open:rotate-180"
              xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"
              viewBox="0 0 24 24"
              fill="none"
              stroke="currentColor"
              stroke-width="2"
              stroke-linecap="round"
              stroke-linejoin="round"
            >
              <path d="m6 9 6 6 6-6"></path>
            </svg>
          </summary>
          <p class="mt-2">
            We recommend checking receipt of your request directly with the
            diplomatic-consular mission (by email, phone, or in person), and
            sending, along with your voting request, a request for notification
            about the data entered in the voters' register (described above).
            You can also check your status in the Unified Voters' Register
            yourself, via <a
              class="text-accent-two underline-offset-2 hover:underline"
              href={externalLinks.electoral_roll}
              target="_blank"
              rel="noopener noreferrer"
            >
              upit.birackispisak.gov.rs
            </a>.
          </p>
        </details>

        <details class="group py-3">
          <summary
            class="text-l text-accent-three title flex cursor-pointer list-none items-center justify-between gap-3 marker:content-none [&::-webkit-details-marker]:hidden"
          >
            My ID card or passport is about to expire – what should I do?
            <svg
              class="h-4 w-4 shrink-0 text-accent-two transition-transform group-open:rotate-180"
              xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"
              viewBox="0 0 24 24"
              fill="none"
              stroke="currentColor"
              stroke-width="2"
              stroke-linecap="round"
              stroke-linejoin="round"
            >
              <path d="m6 9 6 6 6-6"></path>
            </svg>
          </summary>
          <p class="mt-2">
            Submit a request for a new document at the nearest
            diplomatic-consular mission in good time. The legal deadline for
            processing the request is 60 days, so it is important not to delay.
          </p>
        </details>
      </div>
    </section>
  </article>
</BaseLayout>
